This resolution framework explains why odds remain compressed despite periodic disclosure-related news cycles. Congressional hearings feature dramatic testimony, investigative journalists publish compelling reports, and former officials make extraordinary claims. Yet none of these events move markets significantly because traders recognize the gap between sensational allegations and formal government confirmation. The market is pricing a very specific bureaucratic outcome, not the underlying question of extraterrestrial existence.

## Comparing Disclosure Odds Across Timeframes

Polymarket hosts multiple disclosure markets with different expiration dates, creating a term structure of alien confirmation probability. The September 30 market trades at approximately half a cent, while the year-end market prices around three cents. This six-fold difference across three months implies that traders see disclosure as roughly equally unlikely in any given month but assign cumulative probability as more time passes. The structure resembles how options markets price tail risk across maturities, with longer durations commanding higher premiums for extreme outcomes.

This term structure creates [arbitrage opportunities](https://polymarket.com/?r=POLYTips) for attentive traders. If the September market somehow resolves YES, the December market necessarily resolves YES as well. Pricing discrepancies between related markets occasionally emerge during volume spikes, though liquidity constraints typically prevent large-scale exploitation. For retail traders, understanding this relationship helps avoid overpaying for redundant exposure across multiple timeframes.

## What Disclosure Markets Reveal About Prediction Market Limitations

Alien confirmation betting exposes the boundaries of prediction market accuracy. These markets excel at aggregating distributed information and expertise. They fail when no meaningful information exists to aggregate. A 3% probability of government disclosure by year-end reflects collective sentiment about an inherently unquantifiable event rather than rigorous probabilistic reasoning. This does not make the markets useless. They reveal the current state of public belief and provide a mechanism for those with conviction to express views with real money at stake.

For traders focused on edge and returns, disclosure markets serve primarily as entertainment or small-stakes speculation. The smart money concentrates where information advantages exist: economic indicators, political developments, and events with historical precedent and expert analysis. Polymarket.tips tracks positioning in these consequential markets, surfacing moments when multiple skilled traders independently reach similar conclusions. The absence of top-trader activity in disclosure markets is itself informative, signaling that professional prediction market participants see little exploitable edge in pricing first contact.

